@startuml
title Key security protocol for Confidential Identities

participant NodeA
participant NodeB
participant NodeC


===RequestKeyFlow ==
NodeA -> NodeB: RequestKeyForAccount
note right of NodeA
  (UUID="123-456", RandomDataA=0010010101)
end note
note right of NodeA
  NodeA requesting a key for account "123-456"
end note

NodeB --> NodeA: RandomDataB = 1101010110
note over NodeB
  NodeB concatenates RandomDataA and
   RandomDataB and signs over it
end note
NodeB -> NodeA: SignedKeyForAccount\n(Key="A592CF",\n SignedRandomData=Sig(0010010101.concat(1101010110)))
note over NodeA
  NodeA concatenates RandomDataA and RandomDataB. 
  Is this the same as what was sent?
end note
note over NodeA
  Is SignedRandomData a signature which corresponds to the key sent?
end note
note over NodeA
  Store mapping: A592CF => NodeB
end note
note over NodeA
  Store mapping: A592CF => UUID="123-456"
end note
===SyncKeyMappingFlow ==
NodeA -> NodeC: SyncKeyMappingFlow

NodeA -> NodeB: SignedTransaction(Participants=[A592CF])\n transaction involves key: A592CF
NodeB --> NodeA: A592CF = ?
NodeA -> NodeB: A592CF = NodeB

NodeB -> NodeC: RequestKeyFlow
note over NodeC
  (Key="A592CF", RandomDataC=1111011010)
end note
NodeC --> NodeB: RandomDataB = 1000010010
NodeC -> NodeB: ProvideKeyFlow(Key="A592CF", \nSignedRandomData=Sig(1111011010.concat(1000010010)),\n RandomDataC=1111011010)
note over NodeC
  NodeC concatenates RandomDataB and RandomDataC. 
  Is this the same as what was sent?
end note
note over NodeC
  Is SignedRandomData a signature which corresponds to the key requested?
end note
note over NodeC
  Store mapping: A592CF => UUID="123-456"
end note
note over NodeC
  Store mapping: A592CF => NodeB
end note

@enduml